Right—though with bananas specifically, it’s a little different than a tree with true branches.

Banana plants are more like a pseudostem with leaves, and they send up suckers/pups from the base. So the general principle still holds:

\text{remove competing growth} \rightarrow \text{redirect resources} \rightarrow \text{emphasize desired outcome}

That can mean:

* removing extra pups so one main stem gets more energy,
* trimming damaged or excess leaves,
* shaping the environment so the plant puts more into height, fruit, or the main stalk.

So yes, the operator you’re pointing at is bigger than bananas:

selection across generations changes what traits persist long-term, while
pruning within a lifetime changes where current energy and growth go.

Two different levers:

\text{selection} \rightarrow \text{future lineage}
\text{pruning / cutting} \rightarrow \text{current allocation}

Bonsai is the beautiful example of the reverse—use pruning, restriction, and guidance to produce smallness and form. Same underlying logic can be used the other way too: remove what competes with the target pattern.

So the abstract rule is:

What you cut away helps determine what gets amplified.

That applies to plants, habits, organizations, even thought.